export const shiurImportPreviewMarkdown = "# Notes for Siman 95 (part 1)\n\nסימן צה\n\n## Introduction to Zman\n\nBez”H, this zman we will learn some of the most common halachos in יורה דעה, including whether you are allowed to use a dishwasher for meat and milk, how to manage a kitchen with one sink, buying canned vegetables without a hechsher, drinking wine that was poured by a non-Jew or non-religious Jew, whether you may leave wine unattended in your home with a non-Jew, and much more! We will begin the zman with siman 95, which discusses one of the most prevalent issues in our kitchens – the status of parve food cooked in a fleishig or dairy pot. Even if you were not with us last zman when we learned the halachos of basar b’chalav, you will still be able to jump in easily to this limud.\n\n## נ״ט בר נ״ט\n\nIn this section, we will learn the halachos of parve food cooked in a fleishig pot and whether you may eat it with cheese. For example, if you make pasta in a fleishig pot, can you later add cheese and heat it up in the microwave? If you fry parve eggs in a dairy pan, can you eat them with leftover potatoes from Shabbos that were baked in a fleishig oven? Are you allowed to fry fish in a dairy pan and place it on a fleishig plate? This sugya is known as “נ״ט בר נ״ט,” as will be explained below, and we will learn it from its original sources in the Gemara through the modern-day posskim.\n\nAs a mini-introduction, the Torah in three places forbids cooking meat and milk together, and included in this prohibition is eating meat and milk that were cooked together. The גמרא פסחים (מד.) teaches that not only is the mixture of actual meat and milk forbidden, but even the mixture and fusion of meat and milk flavor is prohibited. This is known as טעם כעיקר – the flavor of meat/milk has the status of meat/milk itself. Therefore, milk cooked in a clean fleishig pot is forbidden because the meat flavor absorbed in the walls of the pot enters the milk (see גמרא חולין (צז.)). However, the גמרא עבודה זרה (עה:) teaches that flavor absorbed in the walls of a pot only stays potent for 24 hours (while the pot is ben-yomo). After 24 hours, the absorbed flavor becomes pagum – defective, and can no longer forbid food subsequently cooked inside the pot. Therefore, if you cook milk in a fleishig pot that has not been used for 24 hours (eino ben-yomo), the milk is permitted (but the pot needs to be kashered). All of the above is passkined in the S”A in סימן פז-א וסימן צג-א.\n\nIn our siman, we will discuss the status of parve food cooked in a fleishig or dairy pot and whether it may be eaten with the opposite type of food. The גמרא חולין (קיא:) (1) quotes a dispute between Rav and Shmuel regarding the status of hot parve fish placed on a fleishig ben-yomo plate. According to Rav, the fish may not be eaten with dairy because it absorbed meat flavor from the plate. Shmuel passkins that the fish may be eaten with dairy because the meat flavor in the fish is ״נותן טעם בר נותן טעם (נ\"ט בר נ\"ט)״ – lit. “imparts flavor to something which itself imparts flavor.” In other words, the meat flavor in the fish is weakened because it first transferred from the meat to the plate and only afterward from the plate to the fish. When you later eחat dairy together with the fish, this indirect transfer does not create a sufficiently significant fusion of meat and milk flavor that would forbid the fish. The Gemara passkins like Shmuel.\n\nTosfos in זבחים (צו.) ד״ה ואם (2) explains that the prohibition of basar b’chalav takes effect only when both the meat and milk flavor are strong and fuse together (e.g., cooking milk in a fleishig pot). But in our case, the meat flavor in the fish was weakened twice (from the meat to the plate and from the plate to the fish); thus, when the fish is later mixed with dairy, there is no fusion of strong meat and milk flavor. What emerges from here is that the leniency of נ״ט בר נ״ט applies only to basar b’chalav (where the fusion of flavors forbids the food), but if something is inherently forbidden, נ״ט בר נ״ט does not apply. Therefore, if you cook non-kosher meat in a pot, then clean the pot and cook water in it, the water is forbidden and not considered נ״ט בר נ״ט. Furthermore, if you transfer that water to another pot after it cooled off and heat it up, the second pot becomes treif because it absorbed flavor from the non-kosher water.\n\nAlthough we passkin like Shmuel, the בית יוסף (3) quotes a three-way dispute among the Rishonim on how to understand the words of the Gemara ״דגים שעלו בקערה״, which Shmuel permitted, and the דרכי משה quotes a fourth opinion which contains elements of several of the opinions quoted in the בית יוסף.\n\n- ריב״ן quoting his father-in-law, Rashi – Shmuel only allowed eating the fish with dairy when the hot fish was placed on a fleishig plate, but if the fish was cooked or fried in a fleishig pot/pan, it may not be eaten with dairy. He explains that when a hot piece of fish is placed on a cold fleishig plate, only a little bit of flavor is absorbed in the fish – up to a כדי קליפה.[1] However, when the fish is cooked or fried in a fleishig pot/pan, the fish completely absorbs the flavor of the meat and may not be eaten with dairy.\n\n- ספר התרומה, רא״ש וטור – Shmuel even allowed fish that was cooked in a fleishig pot because the meat flavor is weakened 3 times: from the meat to the pot, from the pot to the water, and from the water to the fish. However, fish that was fried in a pan may not be eaten with dairy because it was only weakened twice: From the meat to the pan and from the pan directly into the fish.\n\n- רמב״ם – Shmuel allows eating the fish with dairy even if it was fried in a fleishig pan because the meat flavor was weakened twice: from meat to the pan and from pan to the fish; the cheese was added only after.[2]\n\n- איסור והיתר (quoted by the דרכי משה) – le’chatchilla, if fish was either cooked or fried in a fleishig pot/pan, you should not eat it with dairy (like the ריב\"ן), but if dairy was mistakenly added to it, it may be eaten, even if the fish was fried in a fleishig pan (like the רמב״ם). Furthermore, everyone would agree that you are allowed to place hot fish that was cooked in a fleishig pot/pan on a dairy plate. Lastly, we are only machmir not to eat fish cooked in a fleishig pot/pan with dairy if the pot/pan is ben-yomo (and the meat flavor is still strong). However, if the pot/pan is eino ben-yomo, the fish may be eaten with dairy le’chatchilla.\n\nThe S”A in סעיף א׳ passkins like the רמב״ם that fish which has already been cooked or fried in a clean fleishig pot may be eaten with dairy. However, if the pot/pan was not cleaned properly (and there are still meat particles on them), you may only eat the fish with dairy if there is shishim in the fish against the meat.[3] The S”A in סעיף ב׳ clarifies that the halacha of נ״ט בר נ״ט (that the flavor weakens through multiple transfers) applies only when the parve item is cooked without meat after the pot was previously used for meat, but if the parve item is cooked in the pot together with meat, the parve item absorbs strong meat flavor. Therefore, an egg cooked together with meat may not be eaten with dairy.\n\nThe Rama in סעיף ב' argues on the S”A and passkins like the איסור והיתר that fish cooked or fried in a fleishig ben-yomo pot/pan may not be eaten le’chatchilla with dairy, but if dairy was already added to it, it may be eaten, because in truth, we follow the opinion that נ״ט בר נ״ט is permitted. The פרי חדש (א) and יד יהודה (ט) explain that the prohibition of intentionally adding cheese to the fish is an added הרחקה, a safeguard to the laws of basar b’chalav, but in essence, it is permitted. Therefore, if the food was already mixed with dairy, it is permitted (even if it was intentionally mixed).[4]\n\nThe קיצור שו״ע (ס״ו פ״א-ז) clarifies that although according to the Rama, it is prohibited to intentionally mix parve food cooked in a fleishig pot with dairy (and vice versa), you are allowed to eat parve food cooked in a ben-yomo dairy pot during a fleishig meal, either before or after the meat.\n\nThe Rama adds four more halachos, three of which we will learn now: 1) Fish that was cooked in a fleishig ben-yomo pan may be placed on a clean dairy plate.[5] 2) If the fish was cooked in a parve pan and placed on a fleishig plate, you may le’chatchilla eat the fish with dairy (after removing it from the plate). 3) Fish cooked in an eino ben-yomo fleishig frying pan may le’chatchilla be eaten with dairy (and vice versa).\n\nWhat emerges according to the S”A is that if plain spaghetti was cooked in a fleishig ben-yomo pot, you may le’chatchilla add cheese to the spaghetti. The Rama would forbid deliberately adding cheese, but if cheese was already added, you may eat it. Additionally, if the spaghetti was made in an eino ben-yomo pot, the Rama allows le’chatchilla adding cheese to it.\n\nThe question we need to ask, both according to the S”A and Rama, is whether one is allowed to intentionally cook parve food in a fleishig pot knowing that he will later add cheese to it (according to the S”A, even a ben-yomo pot, and according to the Rama, only an eino ben-yomo pot). This could happen on erev-Shabbos when all your fleishig pots are already being used and you would like to make spaghetti for a spaghetti and meat dish. According to the S”A, are you allowed to deliberately use a ben-yomo dairy pot to cook the spaghetti, and according to the Rama are you allowed to deliberately use an eino ben-yomo dairy pot?\n\nRav Yosef Karo in בדק הבית (4) (the edits he made to his בית יוסף) quotes רבינו ירוחם, who only allows eating the fish with dairy once it was already cooked, but intentionally cooking it in a ben-yomo fleishig pan to be eaten with dairy is forbidden (and in our case, one would not be allowed to cook the spaghetti in a dairy pot knowing it will later be mixed with meat). Rav Yosef Karo argues and passkins that it is permitted even intentionally.\n\nFrom the language of the S”A, ״שנתבשלו או שנצלו״ – “that were already cooked or fried,” it seems that one may not deliberately cook fish in fleishig dishes with the intention of eating it with dairy, and this is how the ש״ך (ג), בן איש חי (קרח שנה ב-יג), and כף החיים (צה-ט) passkin. However, Rav Ovadya in הליכות עולם (ח״ז-עמ׳ עד) (5) passkins that even cooking fish with intention to eat it later with dairy is allowed. In the footnote, he brings many proofs to this, including that the בדק הבית was written by Rav Yosef Karo after he wrote the S”A, and thus he retracted what he wrote in the S”A.[6]\n\nRegarding the Rama, the גר״א (י), as explained by the בדה״ש (ביאורים ד״ה אם היה) (6), writes that the Rama allows cooking parve food in an eino ben-yomo dairy pot even if you intend to later add meat to it. He explains that in general, one is not allowed to cook meat in a dairy eino ben-yomo pot, even though the food will not become forbidden since the dairy flavor is pagum, because Chazal were concerned that you might come to use a ben-yomo pot. However, in our case, even if you cook parve in a dairy ben-yomo pot, the Rama agrees that m’ikar ha’din it may be eaten with meat. Therefore, Chazal would never forbid cooking parve in an eino ben-yomo pot lest you come to use a ben-yomo pot, because strictly speaking, the ben-yomo pot itself is permitted.\n\nThe חכמת אדם (מח-ב) (7) argues that the Rama only allows eating the fish with dairy if it was already cooked in an eino ben-yomo fleishig pot, but you may not intentionally cook it in an eino ben-yomo fleishig pot in order to add dairy to it later. However, the חכמת אדם (מח-ב) passkins that if you do not have other pots or cannot borrow, you may rely on the גר״א. Rav Elyashiv quoted by Rav Forst (p. 162) passkins like the חכמת אדם.\n\nTo summarize:\n\n- Everyone agrees that if dairy was already added to parve food cooked in a ben-yomo pot or pan (after the food was removed from the pot), it may be eaten.\n\n- Everyone agrees that dairy may be added to parve food cooked in an eino ben-yomo fleishig pot/pan.\n\n- S”A – You may add dairy to parve food cooked in a ben-yomo fleishig pot (after the food was removed from pot).\n\n  - ש״ך – You may not cook parve food with intention to add dairy.\n\n  - Rav Ovadya – You may even cook it with intention to add dairy.\n\n- Rama – You may not add dairy to parve food cooked in a ben-yomo pot, but if added it is permitted.\n\n- Rama – Dairy may be added to parve food cooked in an eino ben-yomo fleishig pot/pan.\n\n  - Gra – You may cook the parve food in eino ben-yomo pot with intention to later add dairy.\n\n  - חכ״א – You may later add dairy only if parve food was already cooked in an eino ben-yomo pot, but if you have no other pots, you may rely on the Gra.\n\n- According to the Rama, parve food cooked in a ben-yomo fleishig pot may be placed on a dairy plate.\n\n- Hot parve food cooked in a parve pot and placed on a fleishig plate may be eaten later with dairy (Gemara above quoted by Rama).\n\n| Case | Ben-Yomo Fleishig Pot: Cooking parve food with intention to eat with dairy | Ben-Yomo Fleishig Pot: Already cooked parve food and want to add dairy | Ben-Yomo Fleishig Pot: Cooked parve food and added dairy | Eino-Ben Yomo Fleishig Pot: Cooking parve food with intention to eat with dairy | Eino-Ben Yomo Fleishig Pot: Already cooked parve food and want to add dairy | Eino-Ben Yomo Fleishig Pot: Cooked parve food and added dairy |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| S”A | ש״ך – forbidden. Rav Ovadya permitted | Permitted | Permitted | Permitted | Permitted | Permitted |\n| Rama | Forbidden | Forbidden | Permitted | גר״א – Permitted חכ״א – forbidden | Permitted | Permitted |\n\nPractical Applications\n\nParve ice cream\n\nAs mentioned earlier, the Rama passkins that parve food cooked in an eino ben-yomo dairy pot may be eaten with fleishig, and there is a dispute whether you may intentionally cook in this pot knowing you will eat the food with meat – the גר״א permits and the חכ״א forbids. Many kosher ice cream or chocolate companies produce both dairy and parve products. In Israel, there was an ice cream company that made parve ice cream in eino ben-yomo dairy utensils and marketed as “parve.” The logic was that even the Rama agrees that parve food made in eino ben-yomo utensil may be eaten le’chatchilla with meat and is essentially parve.\n\nHowever, the מנחת יצחק (ח״י-עז:ג) (8) passkined that this was erroneous because according to the חכ״א, the Rama does not allow cooking parve food in an eino ben-yomo pot with the intention of being eaten with the opposite food. When labeling the ice cream “parve,” the hashgacha is implying that it may even be eaten with meat, but this is only true according to the גר״א, and a hechsher should follow the stringent opinion.\n\nThe אהל יעקב (עמ׳ תיד) (9) argues that it may be labeled “parve” because the ice cream company produces the ice cream to be eaten as dessert at the end of the meal or as a stand-alone treat, but it is not made with intention to be eaten with meat. Therefore, it is just like any parve food made in an eino ben-yomo pot without intention to be eaten with the opposite food, in which case the halacha is that it may be eaten with the opposite food le’chatchilla. The חכ״א only forbids when your sole intention while cooking the parve food in an eino ben-yomo pot is to eat it with the opposite food. But if your intention is to eat it alone, even the חכ״א would agree that it may be eaten with the opposite food.\n\nFor this reason, the מגילת ספר (בב״ח לח-ב) (10) allows baking a parve chocolate cake for dessert in an eino ben-yomo fleishig pan, even if you know that you will eat the leftover slices with dairy coffee in the morning. Here too, since your main intention is to bake the cake for dessert and not for it to be eaten with the opposite food, it is like any food that has already been made in an eino ben-yomo pot, where le’chatchilla it may be eaten with the opposite food.\n\nHechsheirim with “Dairy Equipment” symbol\n\nThe OU, OK, KOF-K and COR all give the companies they service the option to put DE on their products. DE stands for “Dairy Equipment” and means that the parve food was made with dairy equipment. For Sephardim, DE foods are 100% parve and may be eaten with meat. However, for Ashkenazim, it would depend on whether the machines used in production are benei yoman or not. If they are benei-yoman, the food may not intentionally be added to meat, and if the food was already added, it may be eaten. However, if the machines are not benei-yoman and the product is not made to be eaten just with meat, the product is essentially parve and may be eaten with fleishig le’chatchilla.\n\nI reached out to a few kashrus organizations to see if they place a DE even on products that are made on eino ben-yomo machines and what other criteria are needed to label their products as DE. Rav Yehuda Rosenbaum from the Kof-K explained that most large companies have their machines running around the clock, so if the company decides to make a non-dairy run, the machines are usually benei-yoman. Sometimes the non-dairy run will last for days, or even weeks, and after the first 24 hours, the machines are eino-benei yoman and the product is essentially parve. Yet, the Kof-K still labels them as DE for two reasons. First, the companies often change their minds in the middle of a non-dairy run and spend a day doing just dairy (depending on demands and availability of ingredients). Second, if the product uses chalav stam, they will always write DE, even if they know it is an eino ben-yomo run, because consumers who do not eat chalav stam consider the machines to be not kosher and would never buy a product made with non-kosher keilim.\n\nThe OU related that many companies run multiple dairy and non-dairy runs in a single day. The OU goes one step further and sometimes labels dairy equipment products with an OU-D even though there is no dairy in it. Rav Eli Gersten from the OU explained that in order to get an OU-DE hechsher, a mashgiach needs to come in and make sure the machine is clean[7] (even though it doesn’t need to be kashered) and many companies prefer to skip this step and have their products labeled as D.\n\nFor example, plain Oreo cookies in the US do not contain dairy but are made on dairy equipment (and should not be eaten with meat). Nevertheless, they are marked OU-D because the company wants the ability to run multiple runs a day of dairy Oreos and non-dairy Oreos, without having to call in a mashgiach each time for the non-dairy run.\n\nThe OU also related that certain companies prefer to keep an OU-D even on their fully parve products because they want to have uniform symbols on their packaging for aesthetic and marketing reasons. For example, all Snyder’s pretzels are marked OU-D even though many of their products do not contain any dairy, and some are not even made on dairy ben-yomo equipment. An easy way to determine if your Snyder’s pretzels are parve is by looking at the ingredients: if you do not see an allergy warning for milk, they are 100% parve and may be eaten together with steak. If you do, then you must assume they either contain dairy or were made on dairy equipment.\n\nHowever, it should be noted that for other products, the lack of a milk allergy warning does not necessarily verify that there is no milk in the product. The cRc (Chicago Rabbinical Council) writes: “There are times when something is 100% bona fide milchigs, but it is not labeled as dairy in the allergy panel. This is because only certain components of the milk trigger common dairy allergies. However, as far as kosher is concerned, the food is decidedly dairy. (A similar example would be wheat that has been modified so that it lacks the elements that are problematic for people who are gluten intolerant. Thus, it can be entirely gluten free but also entirely chametzdik. This is one reason why not everything that is gluten-free is kosher for Pesach.)”\n\n## The Rama’s Leniencies\n\nIn this section, we will delve into a few of the Rama’s leniencies regarding נ״ט בר נ״ט.\n\nCooking gefilte fish for Shabbos in dairy pots\n\nWe learned in the previous section that although the Rama forbids le’chatchilla adding dairy to parve food cooked in a ben-yomo fleishig pot, he allows placing the food on a dairy plate.[8] Here, too, we must ask whether, according to the Rama, you are allowed to intentionally cook parve food in a ben-yomo pot knowing you will serve it in a utensil of the opposite type. For example, are you allowed to cook or fry fish in a dairy ben-yomo pot/pan on erev-Shabbos, when you are short on pots, knowing that you will later serve it hot on fleishig dishes?\n\nRav Moshe in אגרו״מ (יו״ד ח״ג-י:ב) (11) quotes a dispute on the matter: according to the ש״ך (ג) (quoted earlier) and ט״ז (ד), it is permitted (because, as they imply, the Rama was only machmir not to eat fish cooked in a fleishig pot with dairy food, but would allow placing on a dairy plate), while the פמ״ג forbids. Lema’ase, Rav Moshe is lenient and in the above situation, you are allowed to deliberately fry fish in a dairy ben-yomo pot and serve it on fleishig dishes.\n\nHeating up parve food in a clean fleishig pot that was previously cooked in a dairy pot\n\nWe learned above that even the Rama agrees that you may place hot parve food previously cooked in a ben-yomo fleishig pot on a dairy plate (and vice versa). Additionally, we just learned in the previous subsection that you may even intentionally cook parve food in a fleishig ben-yomo pot, even if you plan to serve it on dairy dishes. What is the halacha if that dairy plate is hot, or better yet, if you want to heat up the parve food in a clean dairy pot? For example, if cold plain spaghetti was made in a ben-yomo fleishig pot, are you allowed to heat it up in a dairy ben-yomo pot?[9]\n\nThe ערה״ש (יג) (12) passkins that you may place hot parve food cooked in a ben-yomo fleishig pot on a hot clean dairy plate (or even in a hot dairy pot), since the meat flavor in the food is נ״ט בר נ״ט and the dairy flavor in the pot cannot forbid it. The ערה״ש acknowledges that he is arguing on the ט״ז (ג), who passkins that parve fish cooked in a fleishig pot may not le’chatchilla be placed on a hot dairy plate, because the נ״ט בר נ״ט flavor in the fish comes in contact with dairy flavor from the hot plate before it is weakened (but if it was already placed on the hot dairy plate, it would be permitted, since even adding cheese itself does not forbid the food be’dieved).\n\nMixing נ״ט בר נ״ט with one another\n\nIt seems from the S”A and Rama that we are only stringent with נ״ט בר נ״ט when parve food was cooked in a ben yomo fleishig pot and we want to eat it with cheese (or vice versa). However, eating parve food cooked in a fleishig pot with other parve food cooked in a dairy pot is permitted. Therefore, if on a Sunday morning you cook parve eggs (i.e., without cheese) in a dairy ben-yomo pan, you are allowed to eat them with leftover potatoes from Shabbos that were cooked in a fleishig ben-yomo pot.[10]\n\nFrying davar charif in an eino ben-yomo pan\n\nWe learned above that according to the Rama, parve food cooked in an eino ben-yomo pot/pan may be eaten le’chatchilla with dairy. However, the Rama adds a caveat to this halacha: if the food being cooked is a davar charif (sharp food, such as an onion, garlic, or radish), it may not be eaten with dairy, because the sharp food awakens and improves the absorbed pagum flavor in the pot. In other words, whenever you cook parve food in a fleishig pot, the meat flavor which enters the parve food is weakened twice – first when it travels from the meat to the pot, and again when it travels from the pot to the food. However, when the parve food being cooked is a davar charif, the sharp food has the ability to strengthen and awaken the weakened meat flavor absorbed in the walls of the pot, and that flavor remains strong within the sharp food.\n\nFor example, if you fry onions in an eino ben-yomo fleishig pan[11] with intent to use them for a kugel and some of those onions are left over, you may not use those onions with cheese. The reason is that the meat flavor traveled from the meat to the pan and from the pan to the onions, and it did not weaken in the transfer from the pan to the onion due to the onion’s sharp taste. The Rama adds that if you mixed those onions with dairy, the dairy food is forbidden unless there is shishim in the food against the meat flavor that was absorbed in the onions. The ט״ז (ו) explains that if you don’t know how much meat flavor was absorbed in them, you need shishim against all the onions that were fried.[12]\n\nWhat would the S”A hold in the above situation? The S”A in סימן צו-א passkins that a davar charif can draw out flavor from a ben-yomo utensil even without heat, but only to the depth of a כדי נטילה – a fingerbreadth, which is thicker than a כדי קליפה.[13] For example, if you cut an onion with a ben-yomo dairy knife, the area of a כדי נטילה around where you cut may not be used with fleishig food. However, when cooking with a ben yomo dairy pan, the heat also spreads the flavor throughout the entire davar charif (and not just a כדי נטילה) and the onions may not be eaten with meat (i.e., they are not considered נ״ט בר נ״ט).\n\n## Scenarios of נ״ט בר נ״ט\n\nIn this section, we will examine additional details and scenarios of נ״ט בר נ״ט. For example, does heating up a dairy baby bottle containing milk in a parve pot of water render the pot dairy? Can you use a sous vide for meat and dairy foods? Are you allowed to buy canned green beans or corn with no additives without a hechsher? Does נ״ט בר נ״ט only work when the flavor goes from the meat to the pot and from the pot to a parve food, or does it also work if it goes from the meat to other food cooked with it, and from that food to another pot? In other words, what is the status of a parve pot that was used to heat up potatoes if the potatoes were previously cooked with meat? We will learn three different principles that apply to נ״ט בר נ״ט and then apply them to the scenarios above.\n\nI. Does נ״ט בר נ״ט apply when flavor does not enter the pot first?\n\nWe learned in the previous section that parve food cooked in a clean, fleishig ben yomo pot has the status of נ״ט בר נ״ט. What is the halacha if, for example, cheese and broccoli are fried in the same pan (on separate sides, and are slightly touching), and then the broccoli alone is heated up in a parve pan? Does the pan become dairy, or is the pan considered נ״ט בר נ״ט (since the cheese flavor was transferred from the cheese to the broccoli and then from the broccoli to the second pan), such that according to the S”A, the second pan remains fully parve (and you may even make it fleishig le’chatchilla), while the Rama would consider the pot to be נ״ט בר נ״ט (and you may not use it for meat, but if you did, the food would remain permitted)?\n\nThis question is subject to a three-way dispute in the acharonim:\n\n- פמ״ג (משב״ז סק״א), quoted in the beginning of פת״ש (א), passkins that נ״ט בר נ״ט applies only if the first transmission of flavor occurs from food into a pot. If the flavor first went into food, e.g., from cheese to broccoli (when they are cooked together), the broccoli is considered dairy. Accordingly, if you later reheat this broccoli in a parve pot, the pot becomes dairy (even according to the S”A) because it is considered as if actual cheese was cooked in it.\n\n- The פני אריה passkins that נ״ט בר נ״ט applies even with two foods. Therefore, if you cook cheese and broccoli together and then cook that broccoli together with a mushroom, the mushroom is considered נ״ט בר נ״ט (from the cheese to the broccoli and from the broccoli to the mushroom). In such a situation, the S”A would allow you to eat the mushroom with meat, while the Rama would only allow it be’dieved (i.e., if the meat was already mixed with the mushroom). The same will certainly apply if you reheat the broccoli in a parve pot, in which case the parve pot is considered נ״ט בר נ״ט.\n\n- The חוות דעת (חידושים-ב) takes a hybrid approach between the פמ״ג and פני אריה and passkins that נ״ט בר נ״ט applies as long as one of the transfers of flavor occurred through a kli. Therefore, if cheese and broccoli are fried in the same pan and then the broccoli alone is heated in another pan, the pan is considered נ״ט בר נ״ט. However, if the broccoli is heated together with mushrooms, the mushrooms are not considered נ״ט בר נ״ט – rather, they become dairy.\n\nHow do we passkin? What is the status of a parve pan that was used to heat up broccoli that was previously fried in the same pan as cheese? The ערה״ש (ג) (13) passkins like the פמ״ג that נ״ט בר נ״ט applies only when the first flavor is transferred into a kli, and the pan used to heat up the broccoli would be dairy. The יד יהודה (הקצר-ב) (14) passkins like the חוות דעת (the hybrid opinion) that as long as one of the flavors was transferred through a kli, it is considered נ״ט בר נ״ט (even if the kli is involved in the second transfer of flavor), and the pan used to heat the broccoli would be נ״ט בר נ״ט. However, he adds an important caveat. If fish is cooked with milk or meat in gravy is cooked with a potato, the fish/potato absorbs actual meat or milk, and not just flavor. Therefore, if the fish/potato are later heated in a parve pot, it is as if you are heating up actual milk or meat in that pot, which would render the pot dairy or fleishig respectively. We are only lenient to consider the second pot נ״ט בר נ״ט when the foods merely touch one another without liquid, as in the case above of broccoli fried in the same pan while touching cheese. Rav Ovadya in הליכות עולם (פ״ז עמ׳ עט) (15) passkins that נ״ט בר נ״ט applies even with two foods, and the pan used to heat the broccoli would be נ״ט בר נ״ט (though it is not clear whether he would permit when the first food absorbed actual milk or meat gravy). In contrast, some Sephardic posskim, such as the כף החיים (צה-י) and בן איש חי (קרח-טו), are machmir like the פמ״ג and ערה״ש.\n\nII. Does נ״ט בר נ״ט apply during cooking?\n\nWhat is the status of parve food cooked in a parve or dairy pot[14] if dairy falls on the outside of the pot? For example, if you are cooking potatoes in a pot and a sizable amount of milk splatters on the outside of the pot (and there isn’t shishim in the pot against the milk), are you allowed to eat those potatoes with meat? Do we say that the milk flavor went from the milk to the pot and then from the pot to the potatoes, thus making the potatoes נ״ט בר נ״ט (such that according to the S”A you may eat them with meat, and according to the Rama they may only be eaten be’dieved if they are mixed with meat), or does נ״ט בר נ״ט not apply during cooking, in which case we assume the milk transfers directly into the potatoes (such that even the S”A would agree that they may not be eaten with meat)?\n\nTowards the end of the פת״ש (א), the חוות דעת is quoted as passkining that נ״ט בר נ״ט does not apply while cooking, and in the above case, the potatoes are considered dairy. The reason is that נ״ט בר נ״ט applies only when there is a break in between the transfers of flavor, which allows the flavor to weaken. For example, in the classic case of נ״ט בר נ״ט, the meat flavor travels from meat to the pot and rests in the pot. When pasta is later cooked in the pot, the meat flavor entering the pasta has already been weakened. In contrast, when the transfer of flavor occurs simultaneously during the cooking process, the flavor is not weakened at all. In contrast, the בית אפרים, also cited by the פת\"ש, argues that נ״ט בר נ״ט applies even during cooking, so the above potatoes are נ״ט בר נ״ט (such that according to the S”A, you may eat them with meat, and according to the Rama, they may only be eaten be’dieved).\n\nHow do we passkin? The מנחת חן (ח״ב יו״ד-ד) (16) passkins that le’chatchilla, Ashkenazim follow the חוות דעת and treat the potatoes as dairy, but if they were accidentally mixed with meat, we rely on the בית אפרים that they have the status of נ״ט בר נ״ט. Rav Ovadya (17) in הליכות עולם (פ״ז עמ׳ עט) passkins le’chatchilla like the בית אפרים that the potatoes are considered נ״ט בר נ״ט.[15]\n\nIII. נ״ט בר נ״ט with prohibitions other than basar b’chalav\n\nWe mentioned in the previous section from Tosfos that the reason נ״ט בר נ״ט is permitted is because the prohibition of basar b’chalav applies only when there is a fusion of both strong flavors (i.e., milk and meat). When one flavor is weakened, there is no fusion of flavors, and נ\"ט בר נ\"ט is permitted. However, if the initial flavor cooked in a pot was itself forbidden, the subsequently weakened flavor always remains forbidden and נ״ט בר נ״ט does not apply. For example, if non-kosher meat was cooked in a new pot and the pot was later cleaned and new water was heated in it within 24 hours, that water is not נ״ט בר נ״ט and will remain forbidden. Even if you were to transfer that water to another new pot and heat it, the second pot becomes forbidden, and if the water is emptied from the second pot and the pot is refilled with clean water, that clean water would also be forbidden.\n\nThe ערה״ש (ב) (18) explains this clearly but adds that if the forbidden flavor eventually dissipates, the food is permitted. To better understand this, let us quickly look at the beginning of סימן צב, where the S”A passkins that if milk falls into meat, you may ask a non-Jewish taster to taste the meat, and if he does not taste any milk, the food is permitted. The Rama argues and passkins that today, we do not rely on non-Jewish tasters and you would need shishim to permit the meat. It should be noted that according to the S”A, a taste test only works when the flavors differ. But if, for example, a bit of non-kosher beef falls into similar-tasting kosher beef, a non-Jew’s tasting does not help to determine the difference, and shishim is needed to permit the kosher beef.\n\nTherefore, in the above case where pork is cooked in a pot followed by water, and that water is then placed in a new pot and heated, the subsequent food cooked in that second pot may be permitted according to the S”A if a non-Jew tastes it and does not taste any pork. Rav Ovadya in הליכות עולם (ח״ז-עמ׳ סב) passkins that today, even Sephardim do not rely on the tasting of a non-Jew unless it is a case of הפסד מרובה – a great financial loss. However, the Rama always forbids because every time water is heated up, it becomes chanan[16] and tasting does not help.\n\nPractical Applications\n\nBaby bottles\n\nWhat is the halacha if one heats up a baby bottle with milk in a parve pot of water (where the water in the pot is yad soledes bo)? The מגילת ספר (סימן לח-א) (19) writes that the status of the pot depends on the dispute between the בית אפרים and חוות דעת mentioned above in subsection II. According to the בית אפרים, the pot is parve because the milk went from milk to bottle, from bottle to water and from water to pot. Thus, there were three transfers and weakening of the dairy flavor before it entered the pot, so both S”A and Rama would agree the pot remains parve. The חוות דעת, however, passkins that the water absorbs the milk flavor directly, so the pot becomes dairy. As mentioned above, Ashkenazim follow the חוות דעת and Rav Ovadya follows the בית אפרים.\n\nChallah heated on a cholent pot\n\nIf you place challah directly on the lid of a cholent pot (without foil), according to the חוות דעת the meat flavor transfers directly into the challah and נ״ט בר נ״ט does not apply since the entire transfer occurs during the cooking process. Therefore, you would not be allowed to eat the challah with dairy.[17] Even according to the בית אפרים, the challah is considered נ״ט בר נ״ט, and the Rama would not permit intentionally eating it with dairy. To allow eating the challah with dairy whenever heating it on top of a pot, you should first wrap the bottom[18] of the challah with aluminum foil. Such a situation is then considered as two pots touching one another[19] (the foil is the second “pot”), and flavor does not transfer between the cholent pot and the challah unless there is liquid between them.\n\nSous vide\n\nIn order to better understand this, and the next couple of subsections, see the following video: bit.ly/SCPsousvide. Today, sous vide has become a very popular cooking method in which a pot or bowl is filled with water and is kept at a specific temperature using a heating rod. Vacuum-packed foods such as meat or fish are then cooked while submerged in the water. If you like sous-viding dairy (e.g., fish with butter or other dairy foods), may you also use it to cook meat?\n\nIt would seem that this question is analogous to the baby bottle in the previous subsection. Therefore, Ashkenazim should be machmir to buy two, because when dairy is cooked with a sous vide, the dairy flavor transfers directly into the heating rod and if you later cook meat, the meat flavor will become basar b’chalav, thereby forbidding both the water and the meat. However, according to Rav Ovadya, who holds that נ״ט בר נ״ט applies even while cooking, one sous vide may be used for both meat and dairy.\n\nWhat emerges from here is that a Sephardi can use a sous vide in a parve pot for cooking dairy, empty the water, and immediately use the sous vide for cooking meat in the same parve pot. In contrast, Ashkenazim may not use one sous vide to cook both dairy and meat, even if the foods are prepared in separate meat and dairy pots. If so, is an Ashkenazi allowed to eat in the home of a Sephardi who sous-vided dairy and then meat? We learned above that according to the מנחת חן (16), if the food was already cooked, even Ashkenazim may be lenient like the בית אפרים and consider the sous vide נ״ט בר נ״ט.\n\nCanned corn and green beans\n\nA common question among travelers and those who live outside of large Jewish communities is whether one is allowed to buy canned vegetables without a hechsher that contain nothing else other than water. At first glance, there would appear to be no problem – how is it different than buying an apple from a local supermarket?\n\nHowever, in truth, we need to get a better understanding of how canned goods are manufactured to properly answer this question (see video above). Canned goods are pasteurized by placing the cans in massive vats filled with water or steam at high temperatures to give them a long shelf life. Often, both kosher and non-kosher meat cans are pasteurized in the vat simultaneously and kosher cans may receive flavor from non-kosher cans (i.e., נ״ט בר נ״ט during cooking – from non-kosher food to can, from can to water, and from water to can of green beans). Furthermore, even if the company is careful not to heat up meat products and vegetables together, the machinery frequently reuses the water/steam, so subsequent vegetable runs may be heated with water that was used to heat up non-kosher cans.\n\nBased on the above, both Ashkenazim and Sephardim prohibit buying canned corn or green beans without a hechsher. The reason is that we learned above that the leniencies of נ״ט בר נ״ט (in contrast to direct absorption) only apply to the fusion of meat and milk, but not to other prohibitions. Therefore, in the first case, when the canned vegetables are cooked together with canned non-kosher meat, the flavor from the meat is absorbed into the vegetables. Even in the second case, when the meat is cooked alone and the water is drained, that water is prohibited according to all, and if it is used to cook the vegetables in the next run, the vegetables become forbidden.\n\nFor this reason, the OU, CRC (Chicago Rabbinical Council), and many other hechsherim recommend against buying canned goods without a hechsher even if they contain only vegetables and water. However, we learned above that be’dieved, Rav Ovadya allows a non-Jewish taster to taste the food, and if he claims that he does not taste any meat, a Sephardi may eat it. According to this, a Sephardi on a business trip who forgot to bring kosher food would be allowed to buy canned vegetables (whose ingredients are only vegetables and water), and if a non-Jew determines[20] that there is no meat flavor in the vegetables, it is permitted. Rav Eli Gersten from the OU pointed out that even if Sephardim do rely on this, it can only be for the specific can that was opened, because other cans on the shelf might have been made earlier in the production line (such as right after a meat run) and may contain more meat flavor.\n\nKosher canned corn or green beans are produced by kashering the machinery and changing the processing water. Alternatively, Rav Eli Gersten explained that instead of changing the water, they often add Britex, classified as the most bitter substance in the world. This is pogem any flavor that might remain in the water.\n\n## Danger of Mixing Fish and Meat\n\nIn this section, we will learn the halachos of the prohibition of eating fish and meat together and whether one is allowed to use fleishig pot or pans to cook fish or grill it on a grill that was just used for meat. Additionally, we will discuss whether Worcestershire sauce, which contains anchovies, may be used as a meat marinade. At first glance, it seems from the sugya above that the only issue is whether fish cooked in a meat pot may be eaten with cheese, but everyone agrees that the fish may be eaten on its own, even though it was cooked in a meat pot. However, it could be that the Gemara was discussing only the issue of נ״ט בר נ״ט and not the possible סכנה – the danger of eating meat and fish together. As usual, we will begin with the earliest sources and work our way down to the modern-day posskim.\n\nThe גמרא פסחים (עו:) discusses the sugya of ״ריחא מילתא היא״ – whether the smell/aroma of a food is considered like the food itself. It is clear that you may not cook meat and milk together, but there is a dispute in the Gemara whether you may bake them in separate trays within the same oven in a manner that only their aromas mix. The S”A in סימן קח-א passkins that le’chatchilla, it is forbidden to create a mixture of ריחא, but be’dieved, if the foods were cooked in the same oven and there was a fusion of aromas, the food is permitted.[21] The above Gemara (20) adds that according to the opinion that ריחא מילתא היא, fish and meat baked in the same oven may not be eaten because the mixture of fish and meat aroma causes bad breath and tzara’as. It can be inferred from the Gemara that since the opinions only disagree concerning a case of baking fish and meat together, presumably both opinions would agree that cooking fish and meat together, which fuses their flavors (and not just their aromas, as with baking) is certainly forbidden due to concern for bad breath and צרעת.\n\nThis conclusion מרדכי (חולין תשנ) (21) passkins that you may not eat fish that was cooked with meat, nor may you eat fish and meat together (even if they were not cooked together). However, you are allowed to eat fish and then meat without washing your hands and mouth in between. The טור (יו״ד קטז) (22) passkins that eating fish and meat together can cause tzara’as and some are even stringent to have separate set of dishes for fish. He notes that his father, the רא״ש, argues on the מרדכי and would wash his hands and mouth in between eating fish and meat.\n\nThe S”A in סימן קטז-ב (23) passkins that one may not eat meat and fish together, and the Rama adds that one should also avoid baking them in the same oven (even in separate pans) because of ריחא, but if one did, they are permitted. The ט״ז (ב) explains that we are lenient after the fact only when they were baked together, where the only possible issue is ריחא, but if they were cooked together, they are forbidden.\n\nThe S”A in סעיף ג׳ (23) there and in או״ח סימן קעג-ב passkins like the טור that one must wash his hands in between eating fish and meat or meat and fish. The Rama writes that the common custom is not to wash one’s hands or mouth, but it is preferable to eat and drink something in between them, which has the same effect as washing.\n\nThe מג״א (קעג-א) (24) writes that today, “it is possible” that the danger of eating fish and meat no longer exists, as the situation may have changed over time and location. Rav Yochanan Wosner suggests in חיי הלוי (ח״ד-מח) that the מג״א was only lenient to permit eating meat and then fish without washing in between, but he would never allow eating them together. However, the דברי מלכיאל (ח״ב-נג) understands that the מג״א even allows eating meat and fish together, and although we do not follow the מג״א entirely, it may be relied upon as a צירוף.\n\nIn order to fully understand this sugya and answer the questions we opened with, there are two more points we need to learn: 1) May one cook fish in a fleishig pot? 2) If fish falls into a pot with meat, is it batel b’shishim, and if so, may one intentionally be mevatel it?\n\nI. Cooking fish in fleishig pot\n\nWhat is the halacha regarding cooking fish in fleishig pots? The ט״ז (ג) in our siman quotes the מהרש״ל that just like you may not cook meat and fish together, you may also not cook fish in a fleishig pot or pan. This is the ruling of the טור (22) quoted above, who writes that one should have a special pot or pan designated for fish. He explains that although the Gemara of ״דגים שעלו בקערה״ only discusses whether fish cooked in a meat pan may be eaten with dairy, implying that the fish may be eaten by itself, that is because the Gemara was only discussing the halacha as it pertains to basar b’chalav, but in fact, the fish alone is also prohibited because of the danger involved. The ט״ז also quotes the איסור והיתר, who permits be’dieved if the fish was already cooked in a fleishig pan. He explains that we assume there is no סכנה from absorbed flavor, as is evident from the Gemara, which only discusses whether the fish may be eaten with dairy and assumes it may be eaten alone.\n\nLema’ase, the בדה״ש (סק״ח ובציונים סקכ״ד) (25) passkins that the custom is to allow using meat pots for fish le’chatchilla. He explains that the או״ה himself allows only in a be’dieved case because the pot might be dirty, but our fleishig pots are clean; therefore, one may cook fish in them le’chatchilla. For Sephardim, the כף החיים (קטז-כ) (26) also permits cooking fish in a fleishig ben-yomo pot le’chatchilla.\n\nII. Is fish batel b’shishim in meat?\n\nThe ט״ז (קטז-ב) quotes a dispute between the איסור והיתר and the מהרי״ל: The איסור והיתר passkins that fish which falls into meat is batel b’shishim, just like any other prohibition. However, the מהרי״ל passkins that חמירא סכנתא מאיסורא – we are more stringent regarding danger than prohibitions. Just as venom from a snake is not batel b’shishim, so, too, fish mixed with meat is forbidden even if there is shishim against the fish. Lema’ase, the פת״ש (קטז-ג) passkins that regarding fish and meat, the halacha of bitul does apply, and if fish falls into shishim of meat, everything is permitted.\n\nYet, there is still a question that needs to be asked: May you deliberately be mevatel a small amount of one that falls into the other? For example, if fish falls into your meat soup and there is not shishim, may you add more meat to be mevatel the fish?\n\nThe S”A in סימן צט: ה-ו (27) passkins ״אין מבטלין איסור לכתחילה״ – you are not allowed to deliberately nullify a forbidden substance by dropping a small amount of it to a large amount of permitted food (e.g., adding a drop of milk[22] to your cholent). This applies to both biblically and rabbinically forbidden food. Furthermore, if biblically forbidden food accidentally falls into a small amount of permitted food, you are not allowed to add more permitted food to nullify the forbidden food. There is, however, a dispute between the S”A and Rama regarding rabbinically forbidden food that accidentally falls into a small amount of permitted food. The S”A allows adding more permitted food to nullify the prohibition, while the Rama forbids. For example, you may not intentionally pour a drop of milk into chicken soup (a mixture that is rabbinically prohibited) even if there is shishim in the pot against the milk. However, if some milk accidentally spills into the chicken soup and there is not shishim, the S”A allows you to add more soup to nullify the milk, while the Rama forbids.\n\nWhat emerges from here is that both the S”A and Rama agree that you may not intentionally mix rabbinically forbidden food in order to be mevatel it; thus, everyone would agree you may not deliberately mix meat and fish with intention to be mevatel one. However, it would seem that if some fish accidentally became dissolved in meat soup and there is not shishim in the soup, the S”A would allow adding more soup to be mevatel the fish (since the prohibition is only rabbinic), while the Rama would forbid.\n\nNevertheless, the פת״ש (קטז-ג) (28) passkins that even the Rama would agree that you may be mevatel the fish that fell into meat soup (and it seems you may even be allowed to intentionally mix meat and fish to be mevatel one). He explains that the prohibition of ״אין מבטלין איסור לכתחילה״ applies only to prohibitions, but regarding danger, once the fish flavor is no longer tasted, the danger is batel and it is permitted. Therefore, once the taste of the fish is nullified, there is no longer a prohibition of meat and fish together. Rav Ovadya in יביע אומר (ח״א-ח) (29) passkins like the S”A; accordingly, once meat and fish are mixed, you may certainly add one to be mevatel the other, even without accepting the understanding of the פת\"ש within the Rama. Moreover, he quotes the עין יצחק (או״ח כד-ב), who understands the S”A that deliberately being mevatel fish in meat or vice versa is also permitted.\n\nTo summarize:\n\n- It is forbidden to eat meat and fish together (and certainly if they were cooked together) because of its dangerous consequences (i.e., foul smelling breath and tzaraas).\n\n- Fish and meat should not be baked together in the same oven, but if they were accidentally baked together in separate pans, you may eat them, because only the aromas mix.\n\n- S”A –You should wash your hands and mouth between eating fish and meat. Rama – although washing is not necessary, it is best to eat and drink something in between.\n\n- מג״א – Today, the prohibition of mixing fish and meat may not apply.\n\n  - חיי הלוי – the מג״א only permitted not washing hands and rinsing mouth in between.\n\n  - דברי מלכיאל – the מג״א permitted even eating them together, and his opinion is used as a צירוף.\n\n- The consensus among acharonim is that fish may be cooked in clean fleishig pots.\n\n- The consensus among acharonim is that a fish and meat mixture can be batel b’shishim (though some are stringent because of the possible danger involved).\n\n- Many acharonim allow actively being mevatel a fish and meat mixture (i.e., adding one of the two ingredients until there is shishim against the other).\n\nPractical Applications\n\nUsing pots, pans and grilling grate for meat and fish\n\nAs mentioned above, most posskim allow cooking fish in ben-yomo fleishig pots and pans as long as they are clean. The same would apply to grilling – you are allowed to grill fish on a meat grate, as long as the grate is clean.[23] In fact, the ילקוט יוסף (או״ה ח״ג פז-פז) (30) passkins that you may even grill fish and meat on the same rack, at the same time, as long as the grill is not covered. If the top is covered, that would constitute a case of ריחא, which is forbidden le’chatchilla, but if it was already done, the food is permitted.\n\nSimilarly, you would be allowed to cut salty/spicy herring with a fleishig knife because even though the sharp spices in the herring draw out the meat flavor from the knife, that is no different than cooking fish in a fleishig pot, which also draws out the absorbed flavor in the pot.\n\nAccidentally cooking meat and fish together\n\nIf meat and fish are accidentally cooked together and there isn’t shishim in one against the other (and there is no way to be mevatel one), the food is forbidden. What is the status of the pot? Does it need to be kashered? The פת״ש (קטז-ג) quotes the תפארת למשה, who passkins that the pot needs to be kashered because the dangerous mixture of meat and fish flavor is absorbed in the pot. This is different than cooking fish in a meat pot which is permitted, because the absorbed meat flavor in the walls of the pot is less potent, but when actual meat and fish are cooked together, the pot absorbs this dangerous flavor, and thus needs to be kashered.\n\nLema’ase, the תשובות והנהגות (ח״ד-קפט) (31) passkins that no one follows this תפארת למשה who forbids the pots. Rather, he quotes others who maintain that all you need to do is wait 24 hours, and then you may use the pot. He adds that if it is hard to wait 24 hours, you can clean the pot, heat up water in the pot, and then empty the water. The next time you would cook in the pot (even immediately after emptying the water), the dangerous flavor in the pot is already weakened and will not cause any harm. Nevertheless, these solutions only work with meat and fish for two reasons. First, regarding fish and meat, it is possible that the danger exists only with a mixture of actual fish and meat and not their flavors. Second, the מג״א writes that perhaps the prohibition of mixing meat and fish does not apply today.[24] The כף החיים (קטז-יג) also passkins that if a pot is used to cook meat and fish, you may use it without hag’alah after 24 hours have passed.\n\nFish that falls into soup\n\nThere was once a husband whose wife asked him to place the gefilte fish into a pot of hot water on the fire. He didn’t pay attention to which pot on the stove had hot water and instead accidentally dropped the fish into the pot of chicken soup, though he quickly realized and removed it. What is the status of the soup and fish?\n\nRav Wosner in קובץ מבית לוי (עניני יו״ד עמ׳ פח) (32) passkins that if the gefilte fish was frozen and you quickly removed it, everything is permitted. However, if it had begun to defrost, you need shishim against the entire fish, and if there isn’t shishim, everything is forbidden. The שו״ת חיי הלוי (ח״ד-מח:ה) (33) adds that if meat juice falls on gefilte fish, the fish is always forbidden even if there is shishim against the juice. This would be similar to the pssak of the S”A in צ״א-ז that if milk falls on meat with crevices, the meat is forbidden because the milk penetrates the meat and remains in air pockets.\n\nLe’chaim in between fish and meat\n\nWe learned above that according to the S”A (23), one must wash his hands and mouth in between eating fish and meat (and vice versa), and that according to the Rama, the minhag is not to wash one’s hands or mouth, but it is best to eat and drink something in between. It seems that only eating and drinking is recommended according to the Rama, but washing hands is not necessary. In contrast, the מאיר עוז (ח״ז-עמ׳ 833) (34) writes that the חזו״א, the Steipler, and Rav Shach would all wash their hands and also drink something in between fish and meat, often liquor. Today, many people have a custom to make a le’chaim between fish and meat and this, as well as the other customs mentioned, are all rooted in this Rama and S”A.\n\nKiddush and Japanese restaurants\n\nAt a kiddush, herring will often be served together with a fleishig cholent, or, if you are eating at a Japanese restaurant, you may be served sushi together with meat and chicken. We just learned that according to Sephardim, you must wash your mouth and hands in between eating them and according to the Rama, it is best to wash your mouth. But are you allowed to place fish and meat on the same table simultaneously?\n\nRav Yochanan Wosner in חיי הלוי (ח״ה נט:ז) (35) passkins that one is allowed to place fish and meat on the same table because one will certainly be careful not to eat them together, since it is dangerous to do so. Additionally, even the prohibition of placing meat and milk on the same table applies only to a table you are eating from, and not a serving table. Therefore, at a kiddush in shul, where the food is placed on a serving table, it would be permitted. However, the כף החיים (קטז סקל״ה) (36) argues that you are not allowed to place fish and meat at the table on which you are eating because we are worried that you might grab a piece of meat and then fish without washing in between. Therefore, in his opinion, when ordering fish and meat at a Japanese restaurant, you must tell the waiter to first serve one and then the other. Rav Ovadya in יביע אומר (יו״ד ח״ו-ט) does not accept the chumra of the כף החיים. He allows placing meat and fish on the same table even when an individual is eating alone and plans to eat both.\n\nLema’ase, most are lenient, allowing meat and fish to be placed on the same table (using the מג״א as a צירוף), but one should be careful not to eat them together and to change utensils between them, because the utensils often have food residue (if you are certain the utensil is clean, you may reuse the same one).\n\nWorcestershire sauce\n\nWorcestershire sauce is a type of sauce containing anchovies (a type of fish) and is often marketed by the manufacturing companies as a meat sauce to be used when preparing meat dishes. Some Worcestershire sauces are marked with a hechsher followed by the word “Fish” and others are not. What’s the difference? Should kashrus organizations be giving a hechsher to such sauce if it is marketed to be eaten with meat?\n\nRav Belsky, one of the posskim of the OU, passkins in שולחן הלוי (פרק כג-א) (37) that if the sauce contains real anchovies at a ratio that is not batel b’shishim, an OU-Fish sign should be placed on the label. If the anchovies are batel b’shishim,[25] a plain OU sign may be used. For this reason, you may find two Worcestershire sauces, one with an OU-Fish label and one with a plain OU label, because the one labeled “Fish” has a percentage of fish that is not batel b’shishim, while the fish in the plain one is batel b’shishim.\n\nRav Belsky explains further that when the fish is batel b’shishim, we rely on the majority opinion that bitul b’shishim[26] applies to a fish and meat mixture, in conjunction with the opinion of the מג״א who permits fish and meat mixtures entirely. Therefore, a plain OU sign may be affixed to the packaging, though he adds that the ingredients should clearly note that the sauce contains fish so those who are machmir not to rely on bitul b’shishim for fish and meat due to possible danger will know not to use it with meat. When the anchovies in the Worcestershire sauce are not batel b’shishim, an “OU-FISH” sign should be affixed to the packaging.[27] Rav Belsky writes that we are not worried that people will use it for meat (even if the sauce is marketed as a meat sauce), either because they will see the fish labeling or, even if they do use it with meat, they may rely on the מג״א who permits.\n\nBased on what we learned above, there might still be a way to use Worcestershire sauce labeled OU-Fish with meat: If there is a way to determine the exact ratio of fish in the sauce, you would be allowed to add other ingredients to the sauce to be mevatel the fish and then use it with meat. Alternatively, if the Worcestershire sauce is added to a meat sauce, the meat sauce can combine together with the other non-fish ingredients in the Worcestershire sauce to be mevatel the fish element.\n\n## Source Notes\n\n[^1]: As we learned last zman, that תתאה גבר cooks up until a כדי קליפה.\n\n[^2]: The reason the Gemara relates of case of ״עלו בקערה״ and not the bigger chiddush of the food being cooked is because it was showing the chiddush of רב, that even if the fish was just placed on a fleishig plate it may not be eaten with dairy.\n\n[^3]: This differs from the ש״ך (פט-יט), who discusses whether eating pareve food cooked in a pot with meat residue renders a person fleishig; here, however, we are addressing whether that food may be eaten with dairy.\n\n[^4]: The פמ״ג (משב״ז-ד) writes that it is forbidden because אין מבטלין איסור לכתחילה, and if you mix the cheese intentionally, there is a קנס forbidding you from eating it.\n\n[^5]: We will learn below that if the food is a liquid (e.g., parve soup), there are some opinions who forbid placing it in the opposite bowl.\n\n[^6]: Rav Ovadya quotes others who hold that the S”A was written after the בדק הבית, but nonetheless, there is no proof from the language of the S”A to the more stringent position, because Rav Karo was merely using the language of the Gemara.\n\n[^7]: If the machine is dirty and there is shishim in the parve product against the dairy residue, the product is still parve (i.e., DE), as the S”A passkins in סעיף א׳, but Rav Gersten explained that often times there is residue on the belt that may be added to the parve product and there will not be shishim against it.\n\n[^8]: We will learn below that this might apply only to solids and not to liquids.\n\n[^9]: If the spaghetti was made in an eino ben-yomo fleishig pot, it may certainly be heated in a ben-yomo dairy pot, because even adding actual cheese is permitted.\n\n[^10]: It would seem that even if the potatoes were served at the fleishig meal, it would still be permitted, because parve food served at a fleishig meal is only forbidden with dairy itself, as we learned last zman that challah may not be eaten with cheese. Here, the potatoes will not be eaten with actual cheese, but only with eggs that were cooked in a dairy pan.\n\n[^11]: The פת״ש (ד) quotes a dispute whether the same would apply to sharp foods cooked in water in an eino ben-yomo pot. Some hold that even the Rama would agree that the flavor is נ״ט בר נ״ט because the meat flavor went from the meat to the pot, from the pot to the water, and from the water to the onion. Since the water is already נ״ט בר נ״ט, the onion may be eaten with cheese. Lema’ase, the בדה״ש (לו) writes that it is best to be machmir even when cooking with water.\n\n[^12]: If the pot has been used a few times, it would seem that you always need shishim against the entire onion because the pot absorbed a lot of meat flavor in the past, and an onion can reawaken all the old meat flavor absorbed in the pot.\n\n[^13]: The פמ\"ג (סימן עו-א) explains that you need to remove the size of the width of your thumb from the food, which is between 2-2.5 centimeters.\n\n[^14]: The moment the pot is shpritzed with milk, it becomes dairy. The question is whether the contents are considered נ״ט בר נ״ט or actual dairy.\n\n[^15]: It is obvious that the pot becomes dairy because the milk splattered on it, but the question is whether you need to kasher it to make it parve again. According to the גר״א mentioned above, the moment the pot becomes eino ben-yomo, it is parve and you can cook parve food in it le’chatchilla to be eaten with fleishig. However, the חכמת אדם would passkin that one should not le’chatchilla use an eino ben-yomo kli of one type to eat with food of the other type, and you would be required to kasher it to return it to its parve status.\n\n[^16]: The S”A in צב-ג passkins that if some milk falls on a piece of meat and the milk is not batel b’shishim, the entire piece is considered a forbidden entity, known as chanan – חתיכה נעשית נבילה. Thus, if that piece of meat falls into another pot of meat, you would need shishim against the entire piece of meat to permit the mixture, and not just shishim against the milk absorbed in the piece. The Rama passkins that chanan applies to all prohibitions; therefore, when water is initially reheated in the pot and there isn’t shishim in the water against the pork that was previously cooked, all the water becomes a ״חתיכת נבילה״ and shishim is needed against all of it. According to the S”A, chanan only applies to basar b’chalav, and if only 2 k’zeisim of pork were cooked, shishim is only needed against 2 k’zeisim.\n\n[^17]: Rav Moshe in אגרו״מ (יו״ד ח״ב-כו) passkins that even though the challah has absorbed meat flavor from the cholent pot, you do not need to wait six hours after eating it. Furthermore, although fleishig bread is forbidden even to eat alone, lest you come to eat with dairy (see סימן צז), it seems that this challah would not be forbidden as fleishig bread because the בית אפרים considers it נ״ט בר נ״ט.\n\n[^18]: Rav Moshe also passkins that completely covering the challah on Shabbos and heating it on the plata or cholent pot is a violation of הטמנה, and it may not even be placed on the heat sources before Shabbos.\n\n[^19]: The Rama writes in סימן צב that two pots that touch each other while hot do not transfer taste if no liquid is involved, though some say one should not le’chatchila create such a situation.\n\n[^20]: It should be noted that there are various halachos regarding how to ask the non-Jew. For example, you may not just ask him straight out; he needs to be מסיח לפי תומו.\n\n[^21]: Assuming there is no issue of זיעה (i.e., the steam did not mix).\n\n[^22]: It should be noted that רע״א (שו״ת רז) discusses whether adding milk to meat is in fact a case of אין מבטלין איסור לכתחילה since the prohibition of basar b’chalav only begins when there is a fusion of meat and dairy flavor, which never happened in this case. Nevertheless, we are using this as an example to easily illustrate the point of אין מבטלין איסור לכתחילה.\n\n[^23]: However, the OU adds: “In practice, however, since it is difficult to clean a grill, it is recommended that the same grill rack should not be used for meat and fish. Either the fish should be double wrapped in aluminum foil or separate grill racks should be used.”\n\n[^24]: This is in accordance with the דברי מלכיאל’s understanding that the מג״א even allowed eating fish and meat.\n\n[^25]: Presumably, the fish is not tasted in this case.\n\n[^26]: It seems from Rav Belsky’s discussion that even though the anchovies were deliberately added, they are still batel b’shishim. This may be for one of three reasons. First, they were not mixed with meat, but rather with other permissible ingredients, and might ultimately not be used with meat. Second, even if the sauce is later added to meat, the intention when using the sauce was not to actively be mevatel them. Lastly, as noted earlier, it seems from the פת״ש (קטז-ג) quoted above that one may intentionally be mevatel something that is forbidden because of סכנה.\n\n[^27]: The OU does acknowledge that some companies prefer to use an OU fish symbol consistently for all their Worcestershire sauces, even if the fish is batel b’shishim (similar to companies that prefer that all their products be labeled OU-D, even if they are only dairy equipment or parve, as discussed earlier in the notes).\n\n\n<!-- extraction-summary\n{\"source\": \"C:\\\\Users\\\\Family\\\\Downloads\\\\Siman 95 (part 1)\\\\Siman 95 (part 1)\\\\Notes for Siman 95 (part 1).docx\", \"blocks\": 143, \"footnotes\": 27, \"tables\": 1}\n-->\n";
